[38;2;124;124;124mWe have a math exam with two papers, total 28 questions, each paper has at least[0m[38;2;124;124;124mone question. Each pupil attempts 7 questions. Each pair of questions was [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mattempted by exactly two pupils. Show that one pupil attempted either 0 or at [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mleast 4 questions in the first paper.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me set up the problem. Let the first paper have $a$ questions and the second[0m[38;2;124;124;124mpaper have $b$ questions, where $a + b = 28$, $a \geq 1$, $b \geq 1$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet $n$ be the number of pupils. Each pupil attempts 7 questions. Each pair of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mquestions is attempted by exactly 2 pupils.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe total number of pairs of questions is $\binom{28}{2} = 378$. Each pair is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mattempted by exactly 2 pupils, so the total count of (pupil, pair) where both [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mquestions in the pair are attempted by the pupil is $2 \times 378 = 756$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mOn the other hand, each pupil attempts 7 questions, contributing $\binom{7}{2} =[0m[38;2;124;124;124m21$ pairs. So $21n = 756$, giving $n = 36$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo there are 36 pupils.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, let's think about the structure. Let $x_i$ be the number of questions pupil[0m[38;2;124;124;124m$i$ attempted from the first paper (so $7 - x_i$ from the second paper). We want[0m[38;2;124;124;124mto show that some $x_i \in \{0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7\}$ satisfies $x_i = 0$ or [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$x_i \geq 4$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mEquivalently, we want to show that it's impossible for all pupils to have $x_i [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\in \{1, 2, 3\}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, let me re-read: "one pupil attempted either nil or at least 4 questions in[0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe first paper." So we need to show there exists a pupil with $x_i = 0$ or $x_i[0m[38;2;124;124;124m\geq 4$. The negation is: all pupils have $x_i \in \{1, 2, 3\}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mAssume for contradiction that every pupil has $x_i \in \{1, 2, 3\}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me count pairs of questions from the first paper. There are $\binom{a}{2}$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mpairs within the first paper. Each such pair is attempted by exactly 2 pupils. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mSo the total count of (pupil, first-paper-pair) is $2\binom{a}{2}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mOn the other hand, pupil $i$ contributes $\binom{x_i}{2}$ first-paper pairs. So:[0m[38;2;124;124;124m$$\sum_{i=1}^{36} \binom{x_i}{2} = 2\binom{a}{2} = a(a-1)$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSimilarly for the second paper:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$\sum_{i=1}^{36} \binom{7-x_i}{2} = 2\binom{b}{2} = b(b-1)$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mAnd for cross pairs (one from each paper):[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$\sum_{i=1}^{36} x_i(7-x_i) = 2ab$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mAlso, $\sum x_i = $ total attempts on first paper questions. Each first-paper [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mquestion is attempted by some number of pupils. Let $r_j$ be the number of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mpupils attempting question $j$. Then $\sum_{j=1}^{28} r_j = 7 \times 36 = 252$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mAlso, for each question $j$, the number of pairs containing $j$ that pupil $i$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mattempts... Actually, let me think about this differently.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor a single question $j$, the number of pupils attempting it is $r_j$. The [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mnumber of pairs $\{j, k\}$ (for $k \neq j$) is 27, and each is attempted by [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mexactly 2 pupils. So $\sum_{k \neq j} (\text{pupils attempting both } j [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\text{ and } k) = 2 \times 27 = 54$. But also, if question $j$ is attempted by [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$r_j$ pupils, each of whom attempts 6 other questions, then $\sum_{k \neq j} [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(\text{pupils attempting both } j \text{ and } k) = 6 r_j$. So $6 r_j = 54$, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mgiving $r_j = 9$ for every question $j$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo every question is attempted by exactly 9 pupils. This is a nice structural [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfact.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTherefore $\sum_{i=1}^{36} x_i = 9a$ (each first-paper question attempted by 9 [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mpupils).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, under our assumption $x_i \in \{1, 2, 3\}$:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$\sum x_i = 9a$, and $\sum \binom{x_i}{2} = a(a-1)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet $n_1, n_2, n_3$ be the number of pupils with $x_i = 1, 2, 3$ respectively. [0m
